<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>io.github.xyz-jphil</groupId>
    <artifactId>xyz-jphil-arcadedb-datahelper</artifactId>
    <!--
      1.4 — closes the delete side, and adds the repair for the failure that not having it caused.
        * Delete: the typed bulk delete, the third verb beside Query (read) and Upsert (write).
          db.command("sql", "DELETE FROM X WHERE ...") does not survive scale: removing ~370,000 rows
          left the LSM indexes holding entries for records that were gone, and every LATER indexed
          read then threw RecordNotFoundException from the middle of an iteration. Delete loads each
          record and removes it through Database.deleteRecord — loading is not incidental, the engine
          needs the record's own property values to know which index entries to remove. It also
          collects the identities in ONE indexed pass first: the obvious SELECT ... LIMIT n loop
          re-scans from the start every round and measured 82 SECONDS to remove 50,000 rows.
        * Indexes: rebuild(), the only repair that works, plus orphans()/names() to see the damage.
          An orphaned entry cannot be fixed by the code that caused it — it has no record to load, so
          a delete cannot find it and a re-insert writes new entries beside it. Deleting and
          re-writing every row of an affected type returns byte-identical orphan counts. Dropping the
          indexes and rebuilding them from the records that exist is what restores agreement.
          Definitions come back from InitDoc.initDocTypes, so index shape stays written down once.
        * Query.skippingUnreadable() / unreadableSkipped(): one orphaned entry currently does not
          degrade a query, it destroys it. This makes it skip and COUNT, so a caller can answer and
          say what it lost. Deliberately does not log — count() and exists() are answered from the
          index and still include orphans, and that disagreement is a useful signal.
        Additive. No annotation-processor change, so no consumer's _A classes regenerate.
      1.3 — closes the read side, which was the one place the library still forced string literals.
        * Query: the typed read counterpart to Upsert. Field and value are bound by the generic
          signature, so property("size").eq().value("big") against a Long column is now a compile
          error rather than a silent mismatch. Covers the engine's whole operator set; states, rather
          than hides, the two limits it inherits (no parentheses/NOT; nested embedded fields are not
          filterable) and REFUSES the latter with a named exception instead of returning empty.
          LAZY BY DEFAULT: Query implements Iterable, so for-each streams rows one at a time and a
          break stops the scan; stream() short-circuits; list() is the single eager terminal and is
          named so at the call site; documents()/vertices() expose the engine's own iterator for bulk
          passes that want no per-row object at all.
        * TypeDef.factory() + SchemaBuilder.factory(X::new): the type definition now carries its own
          zero-reflection constructor reference, so reads read query(db, X.TYPEDEF) rather than
          repeating X::new at every call site. Declared once in the model. A default method returning
          null, so a TypeDef written before 1.3 still compiles and still satisfies the interface — it
          simply gets a message naming both fixes, and the explicit query(db, TYPEDEF, X::new)
          overload remains for that case. No annotation-processor change; no _A regeneration.
        * Traverse: adjacency for a caller that already holds the Vertex. $out/$in/$both address by
          $rid() and therefore spend RID.create(db,rid).asVertex(true) to get a vertex back — pure
          waste for a caller that reached the vertex by an indexed lookup, and a redundant record
          fetch per node over a subtree walk.
        Both are additive. No annotation-processor change, so no consumer's _A classes regenerate.
      1.2 — adds Upsert: a typed, field-level upsert for DOCUMENT and VERTEX types (no string literals,
            field and value bound by generics, complements the whole-object instance DSL).
      1.1 — ArcadeDB 26.x compatibility. Two fixes, both binary-compatibility only, no API change:
        * 26.x removed the RID(BasicDatabase, ...) constructors in favour of static RID.create(...).
          The old calls threw NoSuchMethodError, which disabled $out/$in/$both AND every Link<T>
          resolve and write — the whole transient-reference feature, in both directions.
        * Record creation went through newDocument unconditionally, so the instance DSL
          (obj.in(db).whereEq(..).upsert()) failed on any @ArcadeData(type = VERTEX) with
          "Cannot create a document of type 'X'". A vertex IS a document in ArcadeDB, so creation now
          dispatches on the declared schema type — see NewRecord.
      Published as 1.1 rather than replacing 1.0, which nine other projects still resolve.
      1.5 — enum support (PRP-28 phase 1): a field of an enum type carrying @AsUuid or @AsName is now
        storable, with no annotation on the field itself. ArcadeDoc_I.fromArcadeDocument/fromArcadeMap
        resolve the stored uuid/name string back to the constant via the generated per-field map
        (isEnumField/resolveEnumFromStorage; never throws on an unmatched id — returns null). InitDoc
        maps an enum-typed property to Type.STRING. Document_Update.set/populateEmbeddedDocument/
        dataHelperToMap, Update.whereEq, Query.add (eq/neq/lt/le/gt/ge/between/in) and Upsert.save all
        convert an enum value to its stored string via HasUuid.storageValue before it reaches the
        engine — Upsert.save previously wrote straight to the engine with no such conversion at all
        (an @AsUuid constant would have silently stored its name instead of its uuid); fixed here as
        part of closing this gap. Additive; no schema-property-type change for non-enum fields.
    -->
    <version>1.5</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <maven.compiler.source>21</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>21</maven.compiler.target>
        <arcadedb.version>26.7.3</arcadedb.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- DataHelper Base -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.github.xyz-jphil</groupId>
            <artifactId>xyz-jphil-datahelper-base</artifactId>
            <version>1.1</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- DataHelper Annotations -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.github.xyz-jphil</groupId>
            <artifactId>xyz-jphil-datahelper-annotations</artifactId>
            <version>1.1</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ArcadeDB Engine -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.arcadedb</groupId>
            <artifactId>arcadedb-engine</artifactId>
            <version>${arcadedb.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ArcadeDB Server (for tests) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.arcadedb</groupId>
            <artifactId>arcadedb-server</artifactId>
            <version>${arcadedb.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ASM for bytecode analysis -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.ow2.asm</groupId>
            <artifactId>asm</artifactId>
            <version>9.7</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Lombok -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
            <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
            <version>1.18.42</version>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.11.0</version>
                <configuration>
                    <annotationProcessorPaths>
                        <path>
                            <groupId>io.github.xyz-jphil</groupId>
                            <artifactId>xyz-jphil-datahelper-processor</artifactId>
                            <version>1.1</version>
                        </path>
                        <path>
                            <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
                            <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
                            <version>1.18.42</version>
                        </path>
                    </annotationProcessorPaths>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
